{"aitch":{"id":"98b3b556-972f-48bd-b8da-ee92903de77c","word":"aitch","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"name of the letter H, h","tags":["masculine"],"word":"agá"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"The word hour is written with a silent aitch.","bold_text_offsets":[[39,44]],"type":"example","id":"44b038f2-c58d-41f4-a01d-bfdc3a6691a1"},{"text":"Cockneys drop their aitches.","bold_text_offsets":[[20,27]],"type":"example","id":"30ac0ef4-a7c4-42b9-9a11-f8429262135a"},{"text":"The word length, which contains only four sounds l e ng th, is usually spell'd thus, el ee en gee tee aitch.","ref":"1773 October, The Monthly Review Or Literary Journal Enlarged:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[102,107]],"id":"ddb965d3-b07a-46af-a629-02a0cb6abf77"},{"text":"\"If you've got any drawing-room manners, or a dislike to eating peas with a knife or dropping aitches, you'd better chuck 'em away. They ain't no further use.\"","ref":"1898, H.G. Wells, The War of the Worlds, London: William Heinemann, page 257:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[94,101]],"id":"6cb61e8b-b56d-4a6f-89ea-486c9b7974b7"},{"text":"She frowned, hearing Lim Cheng Po's voice, so English, so refined, so very English upper-class. And often she had had to tell Joe about his aitches.","ref":"1959, Anthony Burgess, Beds in the East (The Malayan Trilogy), published 1972, page 469:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[140,147]],"id":"54a882de-cb01-4d58-838d-d6feb7335b32"}],"categories":["Arabic terms with non-redundant manual transliterations","English countable nouns","English doublets","English entries with incorrect language header","English lemmas","English nouns","English terms derived from Middle English","English terms derived from Old French","English terms derived from Vulgar Latin","English terms inherited from Middle English","English terms with quotations","English terms with usage examples","Entries with translation boxes","Pages with